---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `age = 18

if age = 18:
    print("You are exactly 18.")`;

const Bug2 = `day = 7

if day == 6 or 7:
    print("It's the weekend!")
else:
    print("It's a school day.")`;

const Bug3 = `score = 95

if score >= 60:
    print("You passed.")
elif score >= 90:
    print("You got an A!")`;

const Bug4 = `age = 12

if age >= 16:
    print("You can drive!")
print("Here are the keys.")`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Conditionals</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Four broken conditionals. Only one of them crashes &mdash; the other
            three run happily and give you the wrong answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the debug challenge closest to real programming. Conditional
            bugs almost never announce themselves; the program just quietly does
            the wrong thing.
          </p>

          <h4>Skills to Practice</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>Tracing which branch actually runs</li>
            <li>Spotting an unreachable branch</li>
            <li>Reading indentation as meaning, not decoration</li>
            <li>Writing a condition that says what you meant</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <p>
            For each one, write down which kind of mistake it is, what is actually
            wrong, and the corrected line. Trace it in your head first, then run it
            and see whether you were right.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> refuses to run at all. Python complains about line 3.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug1}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="Bug 1"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The fix is a <b>one-character</b> change. Ask yourself what line 3 is
            currently telling Python to <i>do</i>, versus what it should be asking
            Python to <i>check</i>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Newer versions of Python add a hint to this error suggesting what to
            use instead. Older ones just say <b>invalid syntax</b> and leave you to
            it &mdash; so do not count on the hint being there.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> prints <b class="text-sn-red">It's the weekend!</b>
            &mdash; which looks right, since day 7 is Sunday. But change{" "}
            <code>day</code> to <b>3</b> and it <i>still</i> says it's the weekend.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug2}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="Bug 2"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            That condition reads like English and does not mean what the English
            means. Python sees two separate things either side of{" "}
            <code>or</code> &mdash; what is the second one, on its own?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> a score of 95 prints{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-red">You passed.</b> It never once prints{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-green">You got an A!</b> for any score at all.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug3}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="Bug 3"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Both conditions are written correctly. Nothing is misspelled. The bug
            is in the <b>order</b> &mdash; explain why the second branch can never
            be reached.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> a 12-year-old is correctly told they cannot drive, and
            is then handed <b class="text-sn-red">the keys anyway</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug4}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="Bug 4"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Nothing here is misspelled either, and Python reports no error. The
            whole bug is <b>one line's worth of whitespace</b>. This is the kind of
            mistake that makes indentation worth taking seriously.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Which kind of mistake each one is</li>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Then one more, in a sentence: <b
              >which of these four would be hardest to find in a 200-line program,
              and why?</b
            >
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
